A||||3||Posthumane||Dying Duke||||1||
Z||000000||Posthumane||06-16-2001||01:47 PM||||(84 SE 2.5L M/T) On my way home fom work last night, my engine started crappin out on me. It got really shaky and lost a lot of power, which made me think valve timing, or loose plug wire. However, this morning I noticed the oil level dropped by about 1.5 litres and the coolant level dropped quite a bit too, but only in the radiator (thermostat housing still full). Anyway, I topped up both, started it up for about 15 seconds. Still shakes like crazy, and after you shut it off it makes a sound like it was run w/o coolant, even tho there's plenty in there. There doesn't appear to be any coolant in the oil or vice versa, and it's not leaking out anywhere. There was a bit of moisture in the exhaust tho. Could it be the head gasket? or something else?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Seanh||06-16-2001||02:53 PM||||Could very well be the head gasket. Do a compression test to confirm, but it doesn't sound good from what you said. But then again, if the level was fine at the thermo houseing, and low in the rad, that kinda contradicts a bad head gasket. Was the car on a good level surface when you checked it? Does the car smoke while running, or just start up? What color smoke? Maybe the water pump crapped out on you, and your oil was just low to begin with. Does the engine overheat at all? <P>Compession test would be the first place to start. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||06-16-2001||11:53 PM||||Do a compression test.  If all cylinders are above 100 psig and within 10% of each other then I will be really surprised.<P>84 heads are known to crack when they overheat.  The most common place is right near the rear left head bolt if you look from the back of th car.  Head gasket failure is much more common, so don't worry until you remove the head and have it magna-fluxed.  <P>If you decide to remove the head, you need new head bolts ($25), gaskets ($25), and a valve job ($100).  You can get a rebuilt cylinder head for about $250 if you actually need one.<P>This is a job that is moderate in difficulty and time.  The first head gasket I did on a 84 Duke took me all weekend.  Now, I guess I could do one in a day.  But who likes to work that hard on their day off????<P>Good luck.  Post your compression measurements when you have them.  The problems you are expriencing are pretty common, so the Forum should be a great resource for you.  Search on "head gasket" and see what you find.<P>||||reg||12
Z||000003||theogre||06-17-2001||12:12 AM||||Note: If you have a choice, use the new "Torque To Yeild" head bolts if you remove the head. These are much better than the old bolts that you just torque to XX foot pounds. You will need a good torque wrench and an "angle gauge" to install them. Use the torque rules for an 86 Fiero with these bolts. (Or the ones with the bolt set if they come with instructions.) The rules will say,,, torque bolt #X to YY foot pounds then rotate ZZ degrees. (You work all bolts to the FP specified over at least 2 passes then do the angle on a later pass. don't try to do each bolt in one pass. it doesn't work right like that.) Not all bolts are the same values. the specs are in all the repair books. <P>You can also follow the instructions in the first recall on the 84 4 cyl. Recall 84-C-15B gives the new GM bolt part numbers and the torque info for them. (Links in my cave.)<P>In any case, DO Not reuse the originals. They are known to be weak to start with. It will not suprise me at all if one is broken now or breaks when you try to remove it. The exhaust side one between the 2 & 3 cylinders is a prime pain in the rear.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 06-17-2001).]||||reg||1
